              "question_type": "PYQ",
              "exam_source": "NEET PG 2024",
              "topic": "Action Potential & Ionic Basis",
              "question": "During the rapid depolarization phase (Phase 0) of a ventricular cardiac action potential, which ion channel is primarily responsible for the rapid influx of positive charge?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Voltage-gated fast Sodium (Na+) channels",
                "B": "L-type Calcium (Ca2+) channels",
                "C": "Inwardly rectifying Potassium (K+) channels",
                "D": "Transient outward Potassium channels"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "Phase 0 of ventricular action potential is mediated by opening of voltage-gated fast Na+ channels (Nav1.5), causing rapid influx of Na+. In contrast, nodal tissue (SA and AV nodes) Phase 0 is mediated by slow inward L-type Ca2+ currents.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "Correct. Phase 0 of ventricular action potential is mediated by opening of voltage-gated fast Na+ channels (Nav1.5), causing rapi...",
                  "B": "Incorrect choice for this clinical presentation (L-type Calcium (Ca2+) channels).",
                  "C": "Incorrect choice for this clinical presentation (Inwardly rectifying Potassium (K+) channels).",
                  "D": "Incorrect choice for this clinical presentation (Transient outward Potassium channels)."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Ventricular Phase 0 = Fast Na+ influx. Nodal (SA/AV node) Phase 0 = L-type Ca2+ influx. Resting membrane potential is primarily set by K+ permeability."
              },
