              "topic": "Muscle Spindle vs Golgi Tendon Organ",
              "question": "When a neurologist taps the patellar tendon with a reflex hammer, rapid stretching of the quadriceps muscle activates primary sensory afferents, causing a monosynaptic knee-jerk reflex contraction. Which sensory receptor and corresponding afferent nerve fiber type mediate this dynamic stretch reflex?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Nuclear bag fibers of muscle spindle; Group Ia afferents",
                "B": "Golgi tendon organ; Group Ib afferents",
                "C": "Nuclear chain fibers of muscle spindle; Group II afferents",
                "D": "Free nerve endings; Group IV (C) unmyelinated fibers"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "The classic monosynaptic deep tendon reflex (stretch or myotatic reflex) is initiated by the muscle spindle, specifically dynamic nuclear bag fibers that sense rapid changes in muscle length (velocity of stretch). These signals are carried by Group Ia primary afferents directly to alpha motor neurons in the spinal cord, triggering reflex contraction of the homonymous muscle. In contrast, Golgi tendon organs lie in series with tendons, sense muscle TENSION, and mediate the inverse stretch (autogenic inhibition) reflex via Group Ib afferents.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "Correct. Muscle spindle nuclear bag fibers and Group Ia afferents mediate the dynamic stretch reflex.",
                  "B": "Golgi tendon organs and Group Ib afferents mediate autogenic inhibition (relaxation when tension is excessive).",
                  "C": "Group II afferents innervate static nuclear chain fibers and mediate static muscle length sensing.",
                  "D": "Group IV (C) fibers mediate slow, unmyelinated burning pain."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Proprioceptors Comparison: Muscle Spindle = Parallel with muscle fibers; senses LENGTH and rate of length change; Ia and II afferents; mediates STRETCH REFLEX (contraction). Golgi Tendon Organ = In series at tendon-muscle junction; senses TENSION; Ib afferents; mediates CLASP-KNIFE / INVERSE STRETCH REFLEX (relaxation)."
              },
