              "exam_source": "INI-CET Nov 2022",
              "topic": "Baroreceptor Reflex - Afferent Pathways",
              "question": "A physician performs unilateral carotid sinus massage to terminate a paroxysm of supraventricular tachycardia. Compression of the carotid sinus baroreceptor increases the firing rate of afferent nerve fibers that travel in which cranial nerve to synapse on the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) in the medulla?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX - nerve of Hering)",
                "B": "Vagus nerve (CN X)",
                "C": "Facial nerve (CN VII)",
                "D": "Accessory nerve (CN XI)"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "Carotid sinus baroreceptors monitor arterial blood pressure (sensitive to both absolute pressure and rate of pressure change) and transmit afferent signals via the sinus nerve of Hering, a branch of the Glossopharyngeal Nerve (CN IX), to the Nucleus Tractus Solitarius (NTS) in the solitary tract of the medulla. In contrast, aortic arch baroreceptors transmit afferents via the Vagus Nerve (CN X).",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "Correct. Carotid sinus baroreceptor afferents travel in CN IX (nerve of Hering).",
                  "B": "Aortic arch baroreceptor afferents travel in CN X (vagus). Efferent parasympathetic outflow to the SA node travels via CN X for both.",
                  "C": "CN VII carries taste and visceral motor to salivary glands, not arterial baroreceptors.",
                  "D": "CN XI innervates sternocleidomastoid and trapezius."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Baroreceptor Reflex Pathways: Carotid Sinus = CN IX (Glossopharyngeal, sensitive to hypo- & hypertension). Aortic Arch = CN X (Vagus, sensitive primarily to hypertension). Both terminate in Nucleus Tractus Solitarius (NTS) $\\to$ stimulates parasympathetics (vagus) & inhibits sympathetics."
              },
              "subject_id": "PHYSIOLOGY",
              "subject_name": "Physiology"
