              "id": "PHYS_PYQ_017",
              "question_type": "PYQ",
              "exam_source": "NEET PG 2025",
              "topic": "Coronary Circulation & Myocardial Perfusion",
              "question": "Unlike other systemic vascular beds, myocardial perfusion of the left ventricular subendocardium occurs almost exclusively during which phase of the cardiac cycle, making it the most vulnerable site for ischemic subendocardial infarction?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Diastole",
                "B": "Isovolumetric contraction",
                "C": "Rapid ventricular ejection",
                "D": "Reduced ventricular ejection"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "During ventricular systole, the powerful intramyocardial compressive forces generated by the thick left ventricular wall exceed aortic pressure in the deeper layers, virtually compressing and collapsing the subendocardial capillaries. Therefore, ~80% of coronary blood flow to the left ventricle occurs during DIASTOLE, when the muscle relaxes and coronary vascular resistance drops. Tachycardia shortens diastole disproportionately, severely threatening subendocardial perfusion.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "Correct. Left ventricular subendocardial perfusion occurs predominantly during diastole.",
                  "B": "Isovolumetric contraction produces high intramyocardial tension, halting forward flow in subendocardial vessels.",
                  "C": "Rapid ejection maintains high wall tension that impedes left coronary blood flow.",
                  "D": "Reduced ejection still involves systolic compression."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Coronary Hemodynamics: Left Ventricle is perfused primarily during DIASTOLE (owing to systolic compression). Right Ventricle is perfused during BOTH systole and diastole (lower RV wall tension). Local metabolic factors (Adenosine and Nitric Oxide) are the predominant autoregulators of coronary vascular tone."
              },
              "subject_id": "PHYSIOLOGY",
              "subject_name": "Physiology"
