              "id": "PHYS_PYQ_010",
              "question_type": "PYQ",
              "exam_source": "NEET PG 2022",
              "topic": "Flow-Volume Loops in Obstructive Disease",
              "image_url": "images/flow_volume_loop.svg",
              "question": "A 62-year-old chronic smoker presents with progressive exertional dyspnea. Spirometry demonstrates an FEV1/FVC ratio of 52%. Examination of his maximum expiratory flow-volume loop reveals marked concave scooping during the effort-independent expiratory limb. What is the physiological mechanism causing this characteristic scooped appearance?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Loss of elastic recoil and dynamic compression of small airways during forced expiration",
                "B": "Fixed upper airway obstruction with plateauing of both inspiratory and expiratory loops",
                "C": "Extrinsic chest wall restriction with proportional decrease in all lung volumes",
                "D": "Inspiratory vocal cord dysfunction with normal expiratory curve"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "In obstructive lung diseases such as COPD and asthma, airway narrowing and loss of lung elastic recoil (in emphysema) lead to premature closure of small non-cartilaginous airways during forced expiration (dynamic airway compression). This produces a dramatically reduced expiratory flow rate at mid-to-low lung volumes, seen as a characteristic concave or 'scooped out' appearance of the expiratory flow-volume loop.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "Correct. Dynamic airway compression and loss of tethering radial traction causes expiratory scooping.",
                  "B": "Fixed upper airway obstruction (e.g. tracheal stenosis) truncates and flattens both inspiratory and expiratory limbs.",
                  "C": "Restrictive disease shows a 'witch's hat' loop: normal shape but shrunken proportionally along the volume axis.",
                  "D": "Variable extrathoracic obstruction (e.g., vocal cord palsy) truncates only the inspiratory loop."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Flow-Volume Loop Pearls: Obstructive = Concave scooping of expiratory curve, leftward shift (increased TLC/RV). Restrictive = Tall, narrow 'witch's hat' shape, rightward shift (decreased TLC). Fixed obstruction = Truncated/flat on BOTH inspiration and expiration."
              },
