                "rationale": "Sublingual administration allows the drug to diffuse directly into the capillary network under the tongue, entering the systemic venous circulation via the superior vena cava. This completely bypasses the gastrointestinal tract and the portal-hepatic circulation, preventing first-pass metabolism. This is the physiological rationale for sublingual nitroglycerin in acute angina.",
