              "id": "PHARM_Q01_03",
              "exam_source": "NEET PG 2018 Recall",
              "topic": "Elimination Kinetics - Zero-Order Metabolism",
              "question": "A 30-year-old male with generalized tonic-clonic epilepsy on Phenytoin presents with acute ataxia, nystagmus, and drowsiness following a modest dose increase. Why does Phenytoin exhibit unpredictable toxicity following small dose increments?",
              "options": {
                "A": "It converts from zero-order to first-order kinetics at high concentrations",
                "B": "Hepatic metabolizing enzymes become saturated, shifting elimination from first-order to zero-order kinetics",
                "C": "It is completely excreted unchanged by renal tubular secretion",
                "D": "It has an unusually high Volume of Distribution > 50 L/kg"
              },
              "correct_option": "B",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "Phenytoin displays Michaelis-Menten (saturation) kinetics. At low therapeutic concentrations, metabolism is first-order. However, the hepatic microsomal enzymes (CYP2C9/CYP2C19) become saturated near the upper end of the therapeutic range (10-20 mcg/mL). Once saturated, elimination abruptly shifts to zero-order (constant amount eliminated per unit time). Small incremental increases in dose lead to disproportionate, exponential spikes in plasma drug concentration, rapidly precipitating acute toxicity (nystagmus, ataxia, lethargy).",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "The shift is in the opposite direction: from first-order (linear) to zero-order (saturation) kinetics.",
                  "C": "Phenytoin is heavily metabolized by hepatic CYP enzymes (> 95%), not excreted unchanged.",
                  "D": "Phenytoin has an intermediate Vd (~0.6-0.8 L/kg), not > 50 L/kg."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Classic zero-order saturation drugs: 'PEA' (Phenytoin, Ethanol, Aspirin at toxic doses). Remember: Half-life is NOT constant in zero-order kinetics; it increases as concentration rises!"
              },
