              "id": "PHARM_Q03_03",
              "exam_source": "NEET PG 2018 Recall",
              "topic": "Antianginal - Nitrate Tolerance (Monday Disease)",
              "question": "A patient with chronic stable angina on continuous transdermal nitroglycerin patches notes that the patches have become progressively less effective at preventing chest pain. What is the pharmacological mechanism responsible for this tolerance?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Depletion of tissue sulfhydryl (-SH) groups and generation of reactive oxygen species",
                "B": "Downregulation of vascular beta-2 adrenergic receptors",
                "C": "Induction of hepatic CYP3A4 enzymes",
                "D": "Increased renal clearance of nitrite ions"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "Continuous exposure to organic nitrates causes 'Nitrate Tolerance' (tachyphylaxis). Nitroglycerin must be denitrated by mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH-2) to release nitric oxide (NO), a process requiring reduced sulfhydryl (-SH) groups. Constant exposure depletes intracellular sulfhydryl donors, inactivates ALDH-2, and produces free radicals, blunting cGMP production. To prevent tolerance, a daily 'nitrate-free interval' of 10 to 12 hours (usually overnight) is mandatory.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "B": "Nitrates act via NO-cGMP, independent of beta-2 receptors.",
                  "C": "Tolerance is vascular and enzymatic, not mediated by CYP induction.",
                  "D": "Renal clearance is not the mechanism."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Nitrate Tolerance & Monday Disease: Munition workers exposed to nitroglycerin developed tolerance during the work week; over the weekend, tolerance was lost, so on Monday re-exposure caused severe headache and facial flushing ('Monday Disease'). Prevent tolerance by maintaining a 10-12 hour nitrate-free interval."
              },
              "tags": [
                "Cardiovascular",
                "Nitrates",
                "Tolerance",
                "Monday Disease",
                "NEET PG 2018"
              ],
              "question_type": "PYQ",
              "subject_id": "PHARMACOLOGY",
              "subject_name": "Pharmacology"
