              "topic": "Antitubercular Drugs - Adverse Drug Reactions",
              "question": "A 45-year-old male with pulmonary tuberculosis is started on the standard fixed-dose combination intensive phase regimen (HRZE). Two months later, he complains of burning numbness and paresthesias in both feet, along with loss of pinprick sensation in a stocking distribution. Which drug is responsible for this neuropathy, and co-administration of which vitamin prevents it?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Isoniazid; Pyridoxine (Vitamin B6)",
                "B": "Rifampicin; Thiamine (Vitamin B1)",
                "C": "Ethambutol; Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12)",
                "D": "Pyrazinamide; Nicotinamide (Vitamin B3)"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "Isoniazid (INH) is structurally similar to Pyridoxine (Vitamin B6). INH forms inactive hydrazone complexes with pyridoxal phosphate and promotes its urinary excretion, while also inhibiting the enzyme pyridoxine phosphokinase. The resulting functional Vitamin B6 deficiency impairs GABA synthesis and myelin maintenance, producing a dose-dependent peripheral sensory neuropathy (paresthesias, numbness). Prophylactic administration of Pyridoxine (10-25 mg daily) completely prevents INH-induced peripheral neuropathy.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "Correct. Isoniazid causes peripheral neuropathy prevented by Pyridoxine (Vitamin B6).",
                  "B": "Rifampicin causes red-orange discoloration of secretions, hepatitis, and flu-like syndrome.",
                  "C": "Ethambutol causes retrobulbar optic neuritis (decreased visual acuity, loss of red-green discrimination).",
                  "D": "Pyrazinamide causes hyperuricemia and arthralgias."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "First-Line TB Drugs Toxicities Mnemonic: Isoniazid = Neuropathy (B6 deficiency) & Hepatotoxicity. Rifampicin = Red-orange fluids & potent CYP INDUCER. Pyrazinamide = Hyperuricemia (Gout) & Hepatotoxicity (most hepatotoxic). Ethambutol = EYE toxicity (retrobulbar neuritis, red-green color blindness; optic nerve = cranial nerve 2 = E)."
              },
