              "topic": "Amyloidosis - Staining & Birefringence",
              "question": "An abdominal fat pad biopsy in a patient with nephrotic-range proteinuria and restrictive cardiomyopathy is stained with Congo red dye. Under cross-polarized light microscopy, what pathognomonic optical property is demonstrated by the amyloid fibril deposits?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Apple-green birefringence",
                "B": "Bright yellow fluorescence",
                "C": "Prussian blue granularity",
                "D": "Metachromatic purple coloration"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "Amyloid is an abnormal extracellular proteinaceous substance characterized by a shared beta-pleated sheet tertiary configuration. When stained with Congo red dye, amyloid appears pink or salmon-red under standard light microscopy. However, under cross-polarized light microscopy, the regular alignment of dye molecules along the cross-beta sheet architecture produces a pathognomonic 'apple-green birefringence'.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "Correct. Apple-green birefringence under polarized light after Congo red is the diagnostic hallmark of amyloid.",
                  "B": "Thioflavin T produces yellow-green fluorescence under ultraviolet light, but Congo red polarization is the gold standard.",
                  "C": "Prussian blue (Perls' stain) detects ferric iron deposits in hemochromatosis and hemosiderosis.",
                  "D": "Metachromatic purple with methyl violet is an older less specific screening method."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Amyloidosis Diagnostics: Congo Red stain -> Salmon pink on light microscopy -> APPLE-GREEN BIREFRINGENCE on polarizing microscopy. Electron microscopy shows non-branching 7.5 to 10 nm fibrils in beta-pleated sheet conformation. Abdominal fat pad or rectal biopsy is the preferred initial screening site."
              },
