              "topic": "Hazard Ratio in Survival Analysis",
              "question": "In a randomized trial of an oncology drug, the reported Hazard Ratio (HR) for overall mortality is 0.65 (95% CI 0.50 to 0.84, p < 0.001). How is this result interpreted?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Treatment group has a 35% reduction in the instantaneous risk of death at any given point in time compared to the control group",
                "B": "35% of patients survived",
                "C": "Mortality increased by 65%",
                "D": "Study is statistically insignificant"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "Hazard Ratio (HR) compares the likelihood of an event occurring in the intervention vs control group per unit time. HR = 0.65 represents a 35% relative reduction in the event rate (1 - 0.65 = 0.35 or 35%).",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "CORRECT: Hazard Ratio (HR) compares the likelihood of an event occurring in the intervention vs control group per unit time. HR = 0.65 represents a 35% relative reduction in the event rate (1 - 0.65 = 0.35 or 35%).",
                  "B": "INCORRECT: 35% of patients survived is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                  "C": "INCORRECT: Mortality increased by 65% is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                  "D": "INCORRECT: Study is statistically insignificant is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Hazard Ratio (HR) in Survival Curves (Kaplan-Meier): HR = 1 (no difference); HR < 1 (treatment reduces hazard rate); HR > 1 (treatment increases hazard). Evaluated via Log-Rank test."
              },
