              "question": "A 55-year-old male presents with sudden complete ptosis, outward and downward deviation of the left eye ('down and out'), and a WIDELY DILATED, NON-REACTIVE pupil. What life-threatening vascular lesion must be immediately ruled out by emergency CT Angiography?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Posterior Communicating Artery (PCOM) aneurysm compression",
                "B": "Diabetic microvascular ischemic neuropathy",
                "C": "Myasthenia gravis crisis",
                "D": "Acoustic neuroma compression"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "The pupillomotor parasympathetic fibers run on the SUPERFICIAL, peripheral outer aspect of the Oculomotor (CN III) nerve. Compression by an aneurysm (specifically Posterior Communicating Artery [PCOM] aneurysm at junction of internal carotid) causes early, prominent pupillary dilation and unresponsiveness (Pupil-INVOLVING CN III palsy). In contrast, microvascular ischemia (Diabetes/HTN) affects internal nutrient vessels (vasa nervorum), SPARING the outer pupil fibers (Pupil-SPARING CN III palsy).",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "CORRECT: The pupillomotor parasympathetic fibers run on the SUPERFICIAL, peripheral outer aspect of the Oculomotor (CN III) nerve. Compression by an aneurysm (specifically Posterior Communicating Artery [PCOM] aneurysm at junction of internal carotid) causes early, prominent pupillary dilation and unresponsiveness (Pupil-INVOLVING CN III palsy). In contrast, microvascular ischemia (Diabetes/HTN) affects internal nutrient vessels (vasa nervorum), SPARING the outer pupil fibers (Pupil-SPARING CN III palsy).",
                  "B": "INCORRECT: Diabetic microvascular ischemic neuropathy is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                  "C": "INCORRECT: Myasthenia gravis crisis is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                  "D": "INCORRECT: Acoustic neuroma compression is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Third Nerve Palsy Rule: Pupil DILATED & NON-REACTIVE (Pupil-Involving): Surgical Emergency! PCOM Aneurysm until proven otherwise (order immediate CTA/MRA!). Pupil SPARING: Medical ischemic cause (Diabetes Mellitus, Hypertension)."
              },
