              "topic": "Hearing Loss: Rinne & Weber Tests",
              "question": "A 32-year-old female presents with progressive hearing loss in her right eye. Tuning fork tests (512 Hz) show: Rinne test NEGATIVE in the right ear (Bone conduction > Air conduction), Rinne POSITIVE in the left ear (AC > BC), and Weber test LATERALIZES to the right ear. What type of hearing loss is present in the right ear?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Conductive Hearing Loss in the right ear",
                "B": "Sensorineural Hearing Loss in the right ear",
                "C": "Sensorineural Hearing Loss in the left ear",
                "D": "Bilateral sensorineural hearing loss"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "Tuning Fork Principles: (1) Rinne test compares AC vs BC. Normal / SNHL = AC > BC (Rinne Positive). Conductive hearing loss = BC > AC (Rinne Negative). (2) Weber test lateralizes to the diseased ear in Conductive hearing loss (due to absent masking ambient noise), and lateralizes to the normal better ear in Sensorineural hearing loss.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "Correct. Tuning Fork Principles: (1) Rinne test compares AC vs BC. Normal / SNHL = AC > BC (Rinne Positive). Conductive hearing l...",
                  "B": "Incorrect choice for this clinical presentation (Sensorineural Hearing Loss in the right ear).",
                  "C": "Incorrect choice for this clinical presentation (Sensorineural Hearing Loss in the left ear).",
                  "D": "Incorrect choice for this clinical presentation (Bilateral sensorineural hearing loss)."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Tuning Fork Rules: Rinne Negative (BC > AC) = Conductive Hearing Loss. Rinne Positive (AC > BC) = Normal or SNHL. Weber Lateralizes to diseased ear = Conductive HL. Weber Lateralizes to better/normal ear = Sensorineural HL."
              },
