              "exam_source": "NEET PG 2024 Recall",
              "topic": "STEMI ECG Localization & Coronary Anatomy",
              "question": "A 58-year-old male with long-standing diabetes and hypertension presents to the emergency department with severe crushing retrosternal chest pain radiating to his epigastrium and jaw for the past 2 hours. He has cool, clammy extremities. ECG reveals 3 mm ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF, with reciprocal ST depression in leads I and aVL. Which of the following coronary arteries is the culprit vessel?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Left Anterior Descending (LAD) artery",
                "B": "Right Coronary Artery (RCA)",
                "C": "Left Circumflex (LCx) artery",
                "D": "Left Main Coronary Artery (LMCA)"
              },
              "correct_option": "B",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF is diagnostic of an acute Inferior Wall Myocardial Infarction (IWMI). In approximately 85-90% of individuals with right-dominant coronary circulation, the inferior wall of the left ventricle and the posterior descending artery (PDA) are supplied by the Right Coronary Artery (RCA). ST elevation in lead III > lead II accompanied by ST depression in lead I/aVL strongly points to RCA occlusion rather than the left circumflex artery.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "LAD occlusion causes Anterior / Anteroseptal MI with ST elevation in leads V1 to V4.",
                  "C": "LCx occlusion causes Lateral MI (leads I, aVL, V5, V6) or inferior MI where ST elevation in lead II > lead III.",
                  "D": "LMCA occlusion causes diffuse ST depression with prominent ST elevation in lead aVR (> 1 mm)."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "ECG Lead to Coronary Artery Mapping:\n• Anterior / Septal (V1-V4): LAD ('Widowmaker')\n• Inferior (II, III, aVF): RCA (85%) > LCx (15%)\n• Lateral (I, aVL, V5, V6): LCx or diagonal branches of LAD\n• Right Ventricular MI (V4R): RCA -> Caution: Nitrates and diuretics are CONTRAINDICATED due to severe preload dependence!"
              },
