              "id": "MED_CVS_002",
              "exam_source": "NEET PG 2023 Recall",
              "topic": "Pericardial Disease - Cardiac Tamponade",
              "question": "A 40-year-old female with metastatic breast carcinoma presents with sudden dyspnea and dizziness. Her blood pressure is 84/60 mmHg, heart rate is 118 bpm, and respiratory rate is 26/min. Physical examination reveals distended neck veins up to the angle of the jaw and distant, muffled heart sounds. On inspiration, the systolic blood pressure drops from 84 mmHg to 68 mmHg. What is the diagnosis and the immediate life-saving procedure?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Tension pneumothorax; immediate needle thoracostomy in 2nd intercostal space",
                "B": "Acute massive pulmonary embolism; intravenous thrombolysis with alteplase",
                "C": "Cardiac tamponade; emergency bedside pericardiocentesis",
                "D": "Constrictive pericarditis; elective pericardiectomy"
              },
              "correct_option": "C",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "The triad of hypotension, elevated jugular venous distension (JVP), and muffled/distant heart sounds constitutes BECK'S TRIAD, classic for Cardiac Tamponade. The drop in systolic blood pressure > 10 mmHg during normal inspiration (here, a drop of 16 mmHg: 84 to 68) represents PULSUS PARADOXUS. In malignant tamponade, pericardial fluid accumulates rapidly under pressure, compressing all cardiac chambers and impairing ventricular filling. The immediate definitive life-saving management is urgent emergency Pericardiocentesis (subxiphoid needle decompression).",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "Tension pneumothorax features tracheal deviation to the contralateral side, absent breath sounds, and hyperresonance on percussion.",
                  "B": "Massive PE causes acute RV strain, but does not cause muffled heart sounds or Beck's triad.",
                  "D": "Constrictive pericarditis is a chronic fibrotic condition presenting with pericardial knock, square-root sign, and Kussmaul sign (rise in JVP on inspiration), without acute hypotension/tamponade."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Cardiac Tamponade Triads & Hallmarks:\n• Beck's Triad: Hypotension, Distended Neck Veins, Muffled Heart Sounds.\n• Pulsus Paradoxus: > 10 mmHg drop in SBP on inspiration.\n• ECG: Low voltage QRS complexes + Electrical Alternans (alternating QRS amplitude).\n• Immediate DOC: Emergency Pericardiocentesis."
              },
