              "exam_source": "INI-CET May 2024 Recall",
              "topic": "Inherited Arrhythmia Syndromes - Brugada Syndrome",
              "question": "A 32-year-old previously healthy male is evaluated after experiencing sudden nocturnal unresponsiveness and syncope. There is a family history of unexplained sudden cardiac death in his uncle at age 35. Resting ECG reveals a distinctive 'coved-type' ST-segment elevation of 3.5 mm followed by a negative T wave in leads V1 and V2. An echocardiogram shows structurally normal cardiac chambers. Genetic testing is most likely to identify a loss-of-function mutation in which of the following genes?",
              "options": {
                "A": "KCNQ1 encoding potassium voltage-gated channel",
                "B": "SCN5A encoding alpha-subunit of cardiac sodium channel",
                "C": "MYH7 encoding beta-myosin heavy chain",
                "D": "FBN1 encoding fibrillin-1"
              },
              "correct_option": "B",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "The presentation is diagnostic of BRUGADA SYNDROME (Type 1 ECG pattern: coved ST elevation >= 2 mm followed by an inverted T wave in >= 1 right precordial lead V1-V2). Brugada syndrome is an autosomal dominant cardiac channelopathy. In approximately 20-30% of genotyped cases, it is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SCN5A gene (chromosome 3p21), which encodes the Nav1.5 alpha-subunit of the cardiac voltage-gated sodium channel. This causes reduced inward sodium current (INa) during Phase 0 of the cardiac action potential, creating a prominent transmural voltage gradient in the right ventricular outflow tract (RVOT) epicardium and predisposing to polymorphic VT and ventricular fibrillation (VF). Treatment of choice in symptomatic patients is an Implantable Cardioverter-Defibrillator (ICD).",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "KCNQ1 loss-of-function causes Congenital Long QT Syndrome Type 1 (LQT1, Romano-Ward syndrome, triggered by swimming/exercise).",
                  "C": "MYH7 and MYBPC3 mutations cause Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (HOCM).",
                  "D": "FBN1 mutations cause Marfan syndrome."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Brugada Syndrome High-Yield Essentials:\n• Gene: SCN5A (loss-of-function in cardiac Na+ channel Nav1.5).\n• Diagnostic ECG (Type 1): Coved ST elevation >= 2 mm followed by negative T wave in V1-V2.\n• Provocative Drug Challenge: Unmasked by Class IC sodium channel blockers (Ajmaline, Flecainide, Procainamide).\n• Triggers for Arrhythmias: Fever, heavy meals, alcohol, vagal tone (occurs at rest or during sleep).\n• Definitive Treatment: Implantable Cardioverter-Defibrillator (ICD); Quinidine or Isoproterenol for electrical storms."
              },
