              "image_url": "images/ecg_inferior_stemi.svg",
              "question": "A 58-year-old male presents with crushing retrosternal chest pain radiating to the epigastrium. A 12-lead ECG demonstrates convex 'tombstone' ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF, with reciprocal ST-segment depression in leads I and aVL. Following administration of sublingual nitroglycerin, his blood pressure plummets from 130/80 mmHg to 70/40 mmHg. Which additional diagnostic step and immediate therapeutic action are indicated?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Record right-sided chest leads (V4R) to confirm Right Ventricular Infarction; administer rapid IV Normal Saline bolus",
                "B": "Immediate administration of high-dose intravenous Furosemide",
                "C": "Intravenous beta-blocker (Metoprolol) to reduce myocardial oxygen demand",
                "D": "Immediate infusion of sublingual Nitroglycerin at higher dosage"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF localizes to an Inferior Wall Myocardial Infarction, most commonly caused by thrombotic occlusion of the proximal Right Coronary Artery (RCA). In ~30-40% of inferior STEMIs, proximal RCA occlusion also causes coexisting Right Ventricular (RV) Infarction. The infarcted RV acts as a passive conduit and is strictly dependent on adequate high preload (central venous filling pressure) to maintain forward flow into the left heart. Nitroglycerin causes profound venodilation, pooling blood in systemic veins and precipitously reducing RV preload, leading to severe hypotension and cardiogenic shock. Recording right-sided precordial lead V4R (ST elevation >1 mm) confirms RV infarction. First-line management is aggressive IV NORMAL SALINE expansion; nitrates, morphine, and diuretics are STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "Correct. V4R detects RV infarction; IV saline bolus restores preload and cardiac output.",
                  "B": "Furosemide further depletes intravascular volume and worsens hemodynamic collapse in RV infarction.",
                  "C": "Beta-blockers can worsen cardiogenic shock and heart block in inferior STEMI.",
                  "D": "Nitroglycerin was the inciting trigger of hypotension and is strictly contraindicated."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Inferior STEMI Pearls: Leads II, III, aVF (RCA occlusion). In ~40%, RV infarction coexists. Triad of RV Infarction: Hypotension + Clear lung fields + Elevated JVP. Diagnosis: ST elevation in lead V4R. Management: Aggressive IV ISOTONIC SALINE. Strictly Avoid: Nitrates, Morphine, Diuretics (drop preload) and Beta-blockers (if heart block)."
              },
