              "exam_source": "Extreme Mode (Clinical Master Bank)",
              "topic": "General Medicine Comprehensive",
              "question": "What is the defining characteristic of mixed hemiplegia?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Paralysis of one arm and the opposite leg",
                "B": "A combination of spasticity and flaccidity in the same limb",
                "C": "Paralysis on one side of the body affecting the upper and lower limbs",
                "D": "Weakness on one side of the face and the opposite side of the body"
              },
              "correct_option": "C",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "Hemiplegia literally means paralysis (-plegia) of one half (hemi-) of the body. Mixed hemiplegia is a less common\nterm but generally refers to hemiplegia with a combination of different types of motor signs (e.g., spastic and flaccid), but the\ncore definition of hemiplegia is paralysis of one side. The most specific answer reflecting the term's core meaning is C. Note:\nSome sources use \"mixed hemiplegia\" to refer to crossed patterns like Weber's syndrome (Option D), but the most general\ndefinition is paralysis of one side.",
                "high_yield_pearl": "Key finding in General Medicine: Hemiplegia literally means paralysis (-plegia) of one half (hemi-) of the body."
              },
              "subject_id": "MEDICINE",
              "subject_name": "General Medicine"
