              "id": "EXT_PDF_MED_028",
              "question_type": "EXTREME",
              "exam_source": "Extreme Mode (Clinical Master Bank)",
              "topic": "General Medicine Comprehensive",
              "question": "A young boy has progressive muscle weakness, calf pseudohypertrophy, and uses his hands to push himself up from the floor (Gowers' sign). Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Myotonic Dystrophy",
                "B": "Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy (DMD)",
                "C": "Becker Muscular Dystrophy",
                "D": "Myasthenia Gravis"
              },
              "correct_option": "B",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "This is the classic triad of DMD, an X-linked recessive disorder. Gowers' sign is a specific maneuver where a child\nuses their hands to \"walk\" up their own body from a squatting position due to profound weakness of the hip and thigh muscles.\nCalf pseudohypertrophy occurs because the muscle tissue is replaced by fat and fibrous tissue, making the calves look large but\nfeel rubbery and weak.",
                "high_yield_pearl": "Key finding in General Medicine: This is the classic triad of DMD, an X-linked recessive disorder."
              },
              "subject_id": "MEDICINE",
              "subject_name": "General Medicine"
