              "id": "EXT_PDF_MED_029",
              "question_type": "EXTREME",
              "exam_source": "Extreme Mode (Clinical Master Bank)",
              "topic": "General Medicine Comprehensive",
              "question": "A 25-year-old woman reports fluctuating muscle weakness that worsens with activity and improves with rest. She has ptosis and diplopia that are more pronounced in the evening. Which test would be most helpful in confirming the diagnosis?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Lumbar puncture",
                "B": "Muscle biopsy",
                "C": "Single-fiber electromyography (SFEMG)",
                "D": "Brain MRI"
              },
              "correct_option": "C",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "This clinical history is classic for Myasthenia Gravis (MG). The hallmark is fatigable weakness. While antibody\ntests (for AChR-Ab) are the first step, SFEMG is the most sensitive diagnostic test. It detects impaired neuromuscular\ntransmission by measuring the \"jitter\" (variability in timing) between the firing of two muscle fibers from the same motor unit,\nwhich is increased in MG.",
                "high_yield_pearl": "Key finding in General Medicine: This clinical history is classic for Myasthenia Gravis (MG)."
              },
              "subject_id": "MEDICINE",
              "subject_name": "General Medicine"
