              "exam_source": "Extreme Mode (Clinical Master Bank)",
              "topic": "General Medicine Comprehensive",
              "question": "A patient with a history of heavy alcohol use is hospitalized and develops agitation, tremors, tachycardia, and hypertension 48 hours after his last drink. He is most likely experiencing:",
              "options": {
                "A": "Wernicke's encephalopathy",
                "B": "Hepatic encephalopathy",
                "C": "Alcohol withdrawal syndrome",
                "D": "A panic attack"
              },
              "correct_option": "C",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "This is a classic timeline and symptom cluster for alcohol withdrawal. Chronic alcohol use enhances the effect of\nthe inhibitory neurotransmitter GABA and suppresses the excitatory neurotransmitter glutamate. When alcohol is abruptly\nstopped, the brain is left in a hyperexcitable state, leading to autonomic hyperactivity (tremor, tachycardia, hypertension) and\nagitation.",
                "high_yield_pearl": "Key finding in General Medicine: This is a classic timeline and symptom cluster for alcohol withdrawal."
              },
              "subject_id": "MEDICINE",
              "subject_name": "General Medicine"
