              "question": "A 68-year-old male is brought to the emergency department with sudden-onset right hemiplegia and expressive motor aphasia that started 2 hours ago. Non-contrast brain CT demonstrates no acute intracranial hemorrhage or early ischemic changes (ASPECTS score 10). Blood pressure is 165/95 mmHg, and blood glucose is 120 mg/dL. What is the approved therapeutic time window from symptom onset for intravenous thrombolysis with recombinant tissue plasminogen activator (Alteplase / Tenecteplase)?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Within 4.5 hours of symptom onset",
                "B": "Within 12 hours of symptom onset",
                "C": "Within 24 hours of symptom onset",
                "D": "Within 48 hours of symptom onset"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "In acute ischemic stroke, intravenous thrombolytic therapy with recombinant tissue plasminogen activator (IV Alteplase 0.9 mg/kg or Tenecteplase 0.25 mg/kg) is proven to improve functional neurological outcomes when administered WITHIN 4.5 HOURS of the last known well time (symptom onset), provided a non-contrast CT has excluded intracranial hemorrhage and blood pressure is controlled (<185/110 mmHg). Beyond 4.5 hours, the risk of symptomatic intracranial hemorrhage outweighs the clinical benefit. Endovascular mechanical thrombectomy is indicated up to 24 hours for confirmed large vessel occlusions of the anterior circulation.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "Correct. Intravenous thrombolysis window is strictly within 4.5 hours.",
                  "B": "12 hours is well beyond the IV thrombolysis window.",
                  "C": "Mechanical thrombectomy (not IV thrombolysis) may be performed up to 24 hours in selected patients (DAWN/DEFUSE-3 trials).",
                  "D": "48 hours is managed with antiplatelets and supportive therapy only."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Acute Ischemic Stroke Management: Time is Brain: 1. Urgent Non-Contrast Head CT (Rule out Hemorrhage). 2. IV Alteplase / Tenecteplase: Window is WITHIN 4.5 HOURS. 3. Target BP prior to thrombolysis: < 185/110 mmHg (use IV Labetalol or Nicardipine). 4. Mechanical Thrombectomy: Window up to 24 hours for anterior circulation large vessel occlusion (ICA / M1)."
              },
