              "id": "EXT_PDF_MED_100",
              "question_type": "EXTREME",
              "exam_source": "Extreme Mode (Clinical Master Bank)",
              "topic": "General Medicine Comprehensive",
              "question": "Which of the following is a sign of decompensated chronic liver disease?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Spider nevi",
                "B": "Palmar erythema",
                "C": "Ascites",
                "D": "Gynecomastia"
              },
              "correct_option": "C",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "Chronic liver disease is considered \"compensated\" when the liver is scarred but still able to perform its essential\nfunctions. It becomes \"decompensated\" when signs of liver failure appear. Ascites (fluid accumulation in the abdomen), variceal\nbleeding, hepatic encephalopathy, and jaundice are the major signs of decompensation. Spider nevi, palmar erythema, and\ngynecomastia are signs of chronic liver disease (stigmata) but can be present in the compensated stage.",
                "high_yield_pearl": "Key finding in General Medicine: Chronic liver disease is considered \"compensated\" when the liver is scarred but still able to perform its essential\nfunctions."
              },
