              "exam_source": "Extreme Mode (Clinical Master Bank)",
              "topic": "General Medicine Comprehensive",
              "question": "A patient of Mediterranean descent presents with severe microcytic anemia, and hemoglobin electrophoresis shows a marked increase in HbF and a near absence of HbA. This is characteristic of:",
              "options": {
                "A": "Iron deficiency anemia",
                "B": "Sickle cell anemia",
                "C": "Beta-thalassemia major",
                "D": "Sideroblastic anemia"
              },
              "correct_option": "C",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "Beta-thalassemia is common in Mediterranean populations. In the major\" form (also called Cooley's anemia), there\nis a severe or complete deficiency in the production of beta-globin chains. Since normal adult hemoglobin (HbA) is composed of\ntwo alpha and two beta chains (α2β2), very little or no HbA can be produced. The body tries to compensate by continuing to\nproduce fetal hemoglobin (HbF), which is composed of two alpha and two gamma chains (α2γ2). This results in the characteristic\nelectrophoresis pattern. The anemia is microcytic because the red cells are made with insufficient hemoglobin.",
                "high_yield_pearl": "Key finding in General Medicine: Beta-thalassemia is common in Mediterranean populations."
              },
              "subject_id": "MEDICINE",
              "subject_name": "General Medicine"
