              "id": "INI_MED_GI_001",
              "exam_source": "INI-CET Nov 2023 Recall",
              "topic": "Inborn Errors of Metabolism - Wilson Disease",
              "question": "A 19-year-old male presents with worsening handwriting, dysarthria, resting and wing-beating tremors, and inappropriate emotional lability. Physical examination demonstrates golden-brown pigment deposition at the limbus of both corneas on slit-lamp examination (Kayser-Fleischer rings). Liver enzymes show mild AST/ALT elevation. Which of the following laboratory profiles confirms the diagnosis of Wilson Disease?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Markedly elevated serum ceruloplasmin (> 60 mg/dL) and normal 24-hour urine copper",
                "B": "Decreased serum ceruloplasmin (< 20 mg/dL), increased 24-hour urinary copper excretion (> 100 ug/24 hr), and elevated hepatic copper content",
                "C": "Elevated transferrin saturation (> 55%) and elevated serum ferritin",
                "D": "Decreased alpha-1 antitrypsin phenotype PiZZ"
              },
              "correct_option": "B",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "Wilson Disease (Hepatolenticular Degeneration) is an autosomal recessive disorder of copper metabolism caused by mutations in the ATP7B gene on chromosome 13q. ATP7B encodes a P-type copper-transporting ATPase in hepatocytes responsible for: (1) pumping copper into bile for excretion, and (2) incorporating copper into apoceruloplasmin to form ceruloplasmin. Defective ATP7B leads to failure of biliary copper excretion and failure to form ceruloplasmin. Toxic free copper accumulates in liver, basal ganglia (lenticular nucleus -> dysarthria, chorea, wing-beating tremor), and cornea (Descemet's membrane -> Kayser-Fleischer rings). Diagnostic tri-fold: Low serum ceruloplasmin (< 20 mg/dL), High 24-hour urinary copper excretion (> 100 ug/24 hr), and elevated hepatic parenchymal copper (> 250 ug/g dry weight).",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "Serum ceruloplasmin is characteristically DECREASED (< 20 mg/dL), not elevated.",
                  "C": "Elevated transferrin saturation and ferritin confirm Hereditary Hemochromatosis (HFE gene).",
                  "D": "Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency presents with neonatal cholestasis, cirrhosis, and panacinar emphysema."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Wilson Disease Key Facts for INI-CET:\n• Gene: ATP7B (Chromosome 13q14) - Autosomal Recessive.\n• Cornea: Kayser-Fleischer rings (copper in Descemet's membrane) & Sunflower cataract (copper in anterior lens capsule).\n• First-line Chelating Agents: Trientine (triethylenetetramine - preferred due to fewer ADRs) or D-Penicillamine.\n• Maintenance / Asymptomatic: Zinc acetate (induces intestinal enterocyte metallothionein, which traps copper in mucosal cells and prevents absorption)."
              },
