                "rationale": "Lynch Syndrome (Hereditary Non-Polyposis Colorectal Cancer, HNPCC) is an autosomal dominant cancer predisposition syndrome caused by germline loss-of-function mutations in DNA Mismatch Repair (MMR) genes: MLH1 (50%), MSH2 (40%), MSH6, and PMS2. Defective MMR leads to genomic hypermutability known as Microsatellite Instability (MSI-High). Hallmarks: Early-onset colorectal cancer (mean age 44 years), proximal/right colon predominance, and high risk of extracolonic malignancies (Endometrial carcinoma is the most common extracolonic cancer in women!). Amsterdam II / Bethesda criteria. Surveillance: Colonoscopy every 1-2 years starting at age 20-25 (or 2-5 years before the earliest family diagnosis).",
