              "id": "MED_ENDO_001",
              "exam_source": "NEET PG 2024 Recall",
              "topic": "Diabetes - Diabetic Nephropathy Screening",
              "question": "A 52-year-old male with a 7-year history of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus presents for routine follow-up. He is asymptomatic. Blood pressure is 134/86 mmHg, HbA1c is 7.4%, and serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL. A spot urine test reveals a Urine Albumin-to-Creatinine Ratio (UACR) of 180 mg/g (confirmed on repeat testing 1 month later). Which of the following classes of medications should be initiated to reduce proteinuria and slow the progression of diabetic kidney disease?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) Inhibitor or ARB",
                "B": "Loop diuretic (Furosemide)",
                "C": "Beta-blocker (Atenolol)",
                "D": "Dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker (Amlodipine) monotherapy"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "A spot Urine Albumin-to-Creatinine Ratio (UACR) of 30 to 300 mg/g defines Moderately Increased Albuminuria (formerly microalbuminuria). In diabetic patients, this is the earliest clinical manifestation of Diabetic Nephropathy. ACE Inhibitors (e.g., Enalapril, Ramipril) or ARBs (e.g., Losartan, Telmisartan) are the cornerstone of therapy. They preferentially dilate the renal efferent arteriole more than the afferent arteriole, thereby reducing intraglomerular capillary hypertension and hyperfiltration, decreasing urinary albumin excretion and slowing the decline in GFR.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "B": "Loop diuretics do not have renal-protective or antiproteinuric properties.",
                  "C": "Beta-blockers can mask hypoglycemic warning symptoms (tachycardia, tremors) and do not directly reduce intraglomerular pressure.",
                  "D": "Dihydropyridine CCBs dilate the afferent arteriole and can theoretically increase intraglomerular pressure if blood pressure is not aggressively lowered; they lack the specific antiproteinuric benefits of ACEi/ARBs."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Diabetic Nephropathy Stages & Pearls:\n• Earliest functional abnormality: Glomerular hyperfiltration (elevated GFR).\n• Earliest morphological change: Glomerular basement membrane (GBM) thickening and mesangial expansion.\n• Pathognomonic lesion on renal biopsy: Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules (nodular glomerulosclerosis).\n• First-line Renoprotective Drugs: ACE inhibitors or ARBs, PLUS SGLT2 inhibitors (which constrict the afferent arteriole by restoring tubuloglomerular feedback)."
              },
