                "rationale": "Post-surgical Hypoparathyroidism (accidental devascularization or removal of all 4 parathyroid glands during total thyroidectomy) is the most common cause of acute Hypocalcemia. Hypocalcemia increases neuromuscular excitability by lowering the depolarization threshold of peripheral nerves. Signs of latent tetany: (1) Chvostek's Sign: Mechanical tapping of CN VII trunk over the parotid gland produces twitching of the ipsilateral circumoral and facial muscles; (2) Trousseau's Sign: Carpopedal spasm (flexion of wrist and MCP joints, extension of IP joints, and adduction of thumb - 'obstetrician hand') induced by inflating a BP cuff > 20 mmHg above systolic BP for 3 minutes (more sensitive and specific than Chvostek!). Emergency treatment for symptomatic acute hypocalcemia is SLOW INTRAVENOUS CALCIUM GLUCONATE 10% (1-2 amps in 100 mL D5W over 10-20 mins) under ECG monitoring (risk of bradyarrhythmias).",
