                "rationale": "Focal Segmental Glomerulosclerosis (FSGS) is the most common cause of Nephrotic Syndrome in adults in the US and patients of African descent. Sclerosis involves a portion of the capillary tuft (segmental) in only a subset of glomeruli (focal, starting in the juxtamedullary cortex). Genetic risk: APOL1 (Apolipoprotein L1) homozygous variants G1 and G2 (which historically conferred evolutionary resistance against African sleeping sickness / Trypanosoma brucei rhodesiense). Secondary causes: HIV (HIV-Associated Nephropathy / HIVAN - collapsing FSGS), Heroin abuse, morbid obesity (hyperfiltration), and sickle cell disease. Proteinuria is non-selective and typically STEROID-RESISTANT (~50% progress to ESRD within 5-10 years). FSGS has a high rate of recurrence (> 30-40%) in renal allografts following kidney transplant, mediated by circulating permeability factors (e.g. suPAR).",
