              "correct_option": "B",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "The MENTZER INDEX is calculated as: Mentzer Index = MCV / RBC count. An index < 13 strongly suggests BETA-THALASSEMIA TRAIT (Minor), whereas an index > 13 suggests IRON DEFICIENCY ANEMIA. In thalassemia trait, defective globin synthesis causes microcytosis (low MCV), but the bone marrow compensates with vigorous erythropoiesis, leading to a high normal or elevated RBC count (> 5.0 million/uL). Dividing a very low MCV (64) by a high RBC count (5.8) yields a low index (11.0). In iron deficiency anemia, lack of iron limits both hemoglobin synthesis and total RBC production, resulting in low RBC count and a Mentzer index typically > 13-14.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "Iron Deficiency Anemia presents with low RBC count, high RDW (> 15%), and Mentzer Index > 13.",
                  "C": "Anemia of Chronic Disease typically presents with normocytic normochromic anemia (or mild microcytic), with normal to low RBC count.",
                  "D": "Sideroblastic anemia is characterized by ringed sideroblasts on Prussian blue bone marrow stain and elevated serum iron/ferritin."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Mentzer Index Formula & Cutoff:\n• Mentzer Index = MCV (fL) / RBC count (millions/uL)\n• Mentzer Index < 13: Suggests Beta-Thalassemia Trait (Confirm with Hb Electrophoresis / HPLC -> HbA2 > 3.5%).\n• Mentzer Index > 13: Suggests Iron Deficiency Anemia (Confirm with Serum Ferritin < 15-30 ng/mL, which is the most sensitive lab test for iron deficiency)."
              },
