                "rationale": "Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT Type 2) is a life-threatening, immune-mediated prothrombotic complication of heparin therapy. The '4 T's' Pretest Probability Score: (1) Thrombocytopenia (>= 50% drop in platelet count, typically nadir 40,000-80,000/uL, severe bleeding is rare!), (2) Timing (typically starts 5-10 days after heparin exposure, or < 24 hrs if re-exposed within 3 months), (3) Thrombosis (new venous or arterial thrombosis occurs in 30-50% - 'White Clot Syndrome'!), and (4) oTher causes ruled out. Pathogenesis: IgG antibodies bind complexes of Platelet Factor 4 (PF4) and Heparin. The Fc tail of the IgG antibody cross-links platelet Fc-gamma-RIIa receptors, triggering massive platelet activation, thrombin generation, and endothelial injury. MANDATORY EMERGENCY RULES: (1) STOP ALL HEPARIN IMMEDIATELY (including flushes and LMWH! LMWH cross-reacts 100% and CANNOT be used!); (2) DO NOT START WARFARIN in the acute phase (Warfarin causes rapid protein C depletion, precipitating venous limb gangrene and skin necrosis!); (3) DO NOT TRANSFUSE PLATELETS (fuels thrombosis); (4) INITIATE DIRECT THROMBIN INHIBITOR: Argatroban (hepatically cleared, ideal in renal failure) or Bivalirudin (renally cleared), or Fondaparinux.",
