                "rationale": "Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) is a myeloproliferative neoplasm characterized by clonal expansion of the granulocytic lineage. Triad: (1) Massive Splenomegaly, (2) Striking leukocytosis with all stages of myeloid maturation (myeloblasts, promyelocytes, myelocytes, metamyelocytes, bands, segmented neutrophils) with prominent 'Myelocyte Peak' and BASOPHILIA, and (3) Markedly DEPRESSED Leukocyte Alkaline Phosphatase (LAP / NAP score < 20; in sharp contrast to a benign Leukemoid Reaction where LAP score is markedly elevated > 100!). Cytogenetics: Philadelphia Chromosome, generated by reciprocal translocation t(9;22)(q34;q11), fusing the ABL1 proto-oncogene on Chromosome 9 with the BCR gene on Chromosome 22. The resulting BCR-ABL1 fusion gene encodes a constitutively active p210 oncogenic tyrosine kinase that drives unchecked myeloid proliferation. Targeted Therapy: Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitors (TKIs): IMATINIB (Gleevec - ATP-binding site inhibitor), Dasatinib, or Nilotinib. TKIs have converted CML from a fatal disease into a manageable chronic condition with near-normal life expectancy.",
