                "rationale": "Enteric Fever (Typhoid fever caused by Salmonella enterica serotype Typhi): Clinical Timeline & Diagnostic Algorithm (Mnemonic: 'BASU' for specimen yield): Week 1 (B = Blood culture): Blood culture is the gold-standard diagnostic test of choice in the first week (positive in 80-90%). Symptoms: Step-ladder pyrexia, coated tongue, malaise, and Faget's sign (Sphygmothermic dissociation / Relative Bradycardia: pulse fails to rise 10 bpm per 1 degree C temperature rise). Week 2 (A = Agglutination / Widal test): Widal test becomes positive in the second week (detects antibodies against O [somatic] and H [flagellar] antigens; diagnostic titers in endemic areas: anti-O >= 1:160, anti-H >= 1:160). Signs: 'Rose spots' (faint salmon-colored macules on abdomen/chest), splenomegaly, pea-soup diarrhea or constipation. Week 3 (S = Stool culture): Complications peak in the 3rd week: Transverse ulceration of hyperplastic Peyer's Patches in the terminal ileum leading to severe LOWER GI HEMORRHAGE or catastrophic INTESTINAL PERFORATION and peritonitis. Week 4 (U = Urine culture): Resolution or chronic carrier state (Salmonella persistence in gallbladder in 1-4%, treated with Cholecystectomy + Ciprofloxacin). Drug of choice for uncomplicated enteric fever: Oral Azithromycin (500 mg/day for 7 days) or Ceftriaxone (due to widespread fluoroquinolone resistance in India).",
